    width: 794px; height: 1123px;
    padding: 50px 60px; box-sizing: border-box;
    overflow: hidden; page-break-after: always;
    position: relative;
    font-family: "DejaVu Serif", "Liberation Serif", Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
  }
  .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }
  h1, h2, h3, h4 { font-family: "DejaVu Sans", "Liberation Sans", Arial, sans-serif; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 20px; }
  td { padding: 5px 0; }
  .section-header { font-weight: bold; margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="page">
  <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin: 0;">Form W-4P</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Department of the Treasury</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin: 0;">Internal Revenue Service</p>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">OMB No. 1545-0074</p>
      <h1 style="font-size: 36pt; margin: 0;">2013</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Purpose. Form W-4P is for U.S. citizens, resident aliens, or their estates who are recipients of pensions, annuities (including commercial annuities), and other retirement payments. This form is used to determine the correct amount of federal income tax to withhold from your payments. You also may use Form W-4P to choose not to have any federal income tax withheld from your payments. If you do not have any federal income tax withheld from your payments, you may need to file an extension to pay any taxes owed. For eligible nonresident aliens, use Form W-4P for 2013.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">What to do?</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Complete lines A through G of the <span data-field="form_name">{{ form_name }}</span> Allowance Worksheet. Use the additional worksheets on page 2 to further determine the amount of federal income tax to withhold. If you have more than one income source, you may need to complete more than one income source worksheet. If you do not want any federal income tax withheld from your payments, you can skip the worksheets and go directly to line 6.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Sign this form. Form W-4P is not valid unless you sign it.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">For accuracy, complete all worksheets that apply.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you have more than one income source, you may need to complete more than one income source worksheet. If you do not want any federal income tax withheld from your payments, you can skip the worksheets and go directly to line 6.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Sign this form. Form W-4P is not valid unless you sign it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">A Enter "1" for yourself if no one else can claim you as a dependent.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">B Enter "1" if: <span data-field="dependent_status">{{ dependent_status }}</span> <span data-field="dependent_status_2">{{ dependent_status_2 }}</span> <span data-field="dependent_status_3">{{ dependent_status_3 }}</span> <span data-field="dependent_status_4">{{ dependent_status_4 }}</span></p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">C Enter "1" for your spouse. But, you may choose to enter "0-" if you are married and have either a spouse who has some income subject to withholding or more than one source of income subject to withholding. Entering "0-" may help you avoid having too little tax withheld.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">D Enter number of dependents (other than your spouse or yourself) will claim on your tax return.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">E Enter "1" if you will file as head of household on your tax return.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">F Child Tax Credit (including additional child tax credit). See Pub. 972, Child Tax Credit, for more information.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">G If your total income will be less than $65,000 ($95,000 if married), enter "2" for each eligible child; then less "1" if you have three to six eligible children or less "2" if you have seven or more eligible children.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For accuracy, complete all worksheets that apply.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are single and have more than one source of income subject to withholding or are married and you and your spouse both have some income subject to withholding and your combined income is more than $65,000, use the Multiple Pensions/More-Than-One Income Worksheet on page 2 to avoid having too little tax withheld.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Separate here and give Form W-4P to the payer of your pension or annuity. Keep the top part for your records.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Form W-4P</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Your first name and middle initial</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Last name</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Home address (number and street or rural route)</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">City or town, state, and ZIP code</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%; text-align: right;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Withholding Certificate for Pension or Annuity Payments</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">OMB No. 1545-0074</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Your social security number</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Claim or identification number (if any) of your pension or annuity contract</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Complete the following applicable lines.</h3>
    <ol style="font-size: 10pt;">
      <li>Check here if you do not want any federal income tax withheld from your pension or annuity. (Do not complete line 2 or 3.) <span style="float: right;">&#9744;</span></li>
      <li>Total number of allowances and marital status you are claiming for withholding from each periodic pension or annuity payment. (You also may designate an additional dollar amount on line 3.) <span style="float: right;">&#9744;</span></li>
      <li>Marital status: <span style="float: right;">&#9744;</span> Single <span style="float: right;">&#9744;</span> Married <span style="float: right;">&#9744;</span> Married, but without at higher Single rate.</li>
      <li>Additional amount, if any, you want withheld from each pension or annuity payment. (Note: For periodic payments, you cannot enter an amount here without entering the number (including zero) of allowances on line 2.) <span style="float: right;">$</span></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Your signature <span style="float: right;">&#9658;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%; text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Date <span style="float: right;">&#9658;</span></p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Form W-4P (2013)</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin: 0;">Deductions and Adjustments Worksheet</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Note. Use this worksheet only if you plan to itemize deductions or claim certain credits or adjustments to income.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%; text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page 2</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">1 Enter an estimate of your 2013 itemized deductions. These include qualifying home mortgage interest, charitable contributions, state and local taxes, medical expenses in excess of 10% (7.5% if either you or your spouse was born before January 2, 1949) of your income, and miscellaneous deductions. For 2013, you may have to reduce your itemized deductions if your income is over $300,000 and you are married filing jointly or are a qualifying widow(er); $275,000 if you are head of household; $250,000 if you are single and not head of household or a qualifying widow(er); or $150,000 if you are married filing separately. See Pub. 505 for details.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2 Enter: <span data-field="deduction_amount_1">{{ deduction_amount_1 }}</span> $ <span data-field="deduction_amount_2">{{ deduction_amount_2 }}</span></p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">3 Subtract line 2 or line 0, if zero or less, enter "0-".</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">4 Enter an estimate of your 2013 adjustments to income and any additional standard deduction (see Pub. 505).</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">5 Add lines 3 and 4 and enter the total. (Include any credit amounts from the Converting Credits to Withholding Allowances for 2013 Form W-4P worksheet in Pub. 505).</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">6 Enter an estimate of your 2013 income not subject to withholding (such as dividends or interest).</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">7 Subtract line 6 from line 5, if zero or less, enter "0-".</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">8 Divide the amount on line 7 by 3,000 and enter the result here. Drop any fraction.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">9 Enter the number from the Personal Allowances Worksheet, line G, page 1.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">10 Add lines 8 and 9 and enter the total here. If you use the Multiple Pensions/More-Than-One Income Worksheet, also enter this total on line 1 below. Otherwise, stop here and enter this total on Form W-4P, line 2, page 1.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Multiple Pensions/More-Than-One Income Worksheet</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Note. Complete only if the instructions under line G, page 1, direct you here. If this applies (if you and your spouse if married filing jointly) have more than one source of income subject to withholding (such as one pension, or a job and you have a pension and your spouse works).</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">1 Enter the number from line G, page 1 (or from line 10 above if you used the Deductions and Adjustments Worksheet).</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2 Find the number in Table 1 below that applies to the LOWEST paying pension or job and enter it here.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">3 Find the number in Table 2 below that applies to the HIGHEST paying pension or job and enter it here.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">4 If line 1 is more than or equal to line 2, subtract line 2 from line 1. Enter the result here (if zero, enter "0-") and on Form W-4P, line 2, page 1. Do not use the rest of this worksheet.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Note. If line 1 is less than line 2, enter "0-" on Form W-4P, line 2, page 1. Complete lines 4 through 9 below to figure the correct number of allowances to claim.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">5 Enter the number from line 2 of this worksheet.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">6 Subtract line 5 from line 4.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">7 Find the amount in Table 2 below that applies to the HIGHEST paying pension or job and enter it here.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">8 Divide line 9 by the number of pay periods remaining in 2013. For example, divide by 12 if you are paid every month and you complete this form in December 2012. Enter the result here and on Form W-4P, line 3, page 1. This is the additional amount to be withheld from each payment.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Table 1</h3>
    <table style="font-size: 10pt;">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="width: 30%;">Married Filing Jointly</th>
          <th style="width: 30%;">Married Filing Separately</th>
          <th style="width: 40%;">All Others</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>If wages from LOWEST paying job or pension are:</td>
          <td>If wages from LOWEST paying job or pension are:</td>
          <td>If wages from HIGHEST paying job or pension are:</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$0 - $5,000</td>
          <td>$0 - $8,000</td>
          <td>$0 - $37,000</td>
          <td>0</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$5,001 - $10,000</td>
          <td>$8,001 - $12,000</td>
          <td>$37,001 - $80,000</td>
          <td>1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$10,001 - $24,000</td>
          <td>$12,001 - $16,000</td>
          <td>$80,001 - $175,000</td>
          <td>2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$24,001 - $30,000</td>
          <td>$16,001 - $25,000</td>
          <td>$175,001 - $385,000</td>
          <td>3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$30,001 - $40,000</td>
          <td>$25,001 - $30,000</td>
          <td>$385,001 and over</td>
          <td>4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$40,001 - $50,000</td>
          <td>$30,001 - $40,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>5</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$50,001 - $65,000</td>
          <td>$40,001 - $50,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>6</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$65,001 - $75,000</td>
          <td>$50,001 - $65,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>7</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$75,001 - $95,000</td>
          <td>$65,001 - $75,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>8</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$95,001 - $120,000</td>
          <td>$75,001 - $95,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>9</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>$120,001 and over</td>
          <td>$95,001 - $120,000</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>10</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin: 0;">Additional Instructions</h2>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%; text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page 3</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Caution.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Penalties are for not paying enough federal income tax during the year, either through withholding or estimated tax payments. New retirees, especially, should be aware of these penalties. You may be able to avoid quarterly tax payments if you are receiving a pension or annuity. You may be able to avoid quarterly tax payments if you are receiving a pension or annuity. You may be able to avoid quarterly tax payments if you are receiving a pension or annuity.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Periodic payments.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Withholding from periodic payments of a pension or annuity is figured in the same manner as withholding from wages. If you are receiving a pension or annuity, you may be able to adjust your withholding by using Form W-4P.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you want federal income tax to be withheld, you must complete Form W-4P. If you do not want federal income tax to be withheld, you must check the box on line 1 of Form W-4P.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you do not want any federal income tax withheld from your periodic payments, check the box on line 1 of Form W-4P and follow the instructions in the "What to do?" section of Form W-4P.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Nonperiodic payments.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Withholding on your periodic payments is figured in the same manner as withholding on wages. If you are receiving a pension or annuity, you may be able to adjust your withholding by using Form W-4P.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you do not want any federal income tax withheld from your nonperiodic payments, you must check the box on line 1 of Form W-4P.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Caution.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you submit a Form W-4P that does not contain your correct social security number, the payer may withhold as if you had no social security number. If you submit a Form W-4P that does not contain your correct social security number, the payer may withhold as if you had no social security number.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin: 0;">Withholding From Pensions and Annuities</h2>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%; text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page 4</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Generally, federal income tax withholding applies to the taxable portion of your pension, annuity, pension, or annuity payments. However, some types of payments are not subject to withholding, such as interest, dividends, or capital gains). Consider estimated tax payments using Form 1040-ES, Estimated Tax for Individuals. Call IRS at 1-800-829-1040 for more information.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Distributions.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Distributions that are (a) required by law, (b) one of specified series of equal payments, or (c) qualifying hardship distributions are not subject to federal income tax withholding. For more information, see Pub. 505.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Changing Your "No Withholding" Choice</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Periodic payments. If you previously chose not to have federal income tax withheld from your pension or annuity, you may change your mind and want withholding. Complete Form W-4P and submit it to the payer of your pension or annuity. If you previously chose to have federal income tax withheld from your pension or annuity, you may change your mind and want no withholding. Complete Form W-4P and submit it to the payer of your pension or annuity.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Nonperiodic payments.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you previously chose not to have federal income tax withheld from your nonperiodic payments, you may change your mind and want withholding. Complete Form W-4P and submit it to the payer of your nonperiodic payments.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Payments to Foreign Persons and Payments Outside the United States</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Unless you are a nonresident alien, withholding on the manner described above is required on any periodic or nonperiodic payments made to you. If you are a nonresident alien, you must complete Form W-8BEN or Form W-8BEN-E to claim a reduced rate of withholding. See Pub. 519, U.S. Tax Guide for Aliens, for details. A foreign person should submit Form W-8BEN or Form W-8BEN-E to the payer before receiving any payments.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Statement of Federal Income Tax Withheld From Your Pension or Annuity</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By January 31 of next year, your payer will furnish a statement to you showing the amount of federal income tax withheld from your pension or annuity payments and the total federal income tax withheld during the year. This statement is Form 1099-R, Distributions From Pensions, Annuities, etc., showing the total amount of your pension or annuity payments and the total federal income tax withheld during the year. You must file this statement with Form 1040, U.S. Source Income Subject to Withholding, by March 15 of next year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Privacy Act and Paperwork Reduction Act Notice</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">We ask for the information on this form to carry out the Internal Revenue Service's tax collection and administration functions. The information you provide will be used for tax administration purposes only. The Internal Revenue Service will not disclose your information to any other party without your consent, except as required by law. The Internal Revenue Service may use your information to carry out its tax administration functions. The Internal Revenue Service may use your information to carry out its tax administration functions.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Routine use of this information including giving it to the Social Security Administration, the Department of Labor, and U.S. commonwealths and territories, is necessary to carry out the Internal Revenue Service's tax administration functions. The Internal Revenue Service may use your information to carry out its tax administration functions.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You are not required to provide the information requested on this form to carry out the Internal Revenue Service's tax administration functions. The Internal Revenue Service may use your information to carry out its tax administration functions.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The average time and expenses required to complete and file this form are listed on the instructions for this form. For estimated averages, see the instructions for your income tax return.</p>
  </div>
</div>

